<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0a0a0f">
  <title>Privacy Policy · <%= siteName %></title>
  <link rel="icon" href="<%= siteLogo %>" type="image/png">
  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #0a0a0f;
      --panel: #12121a;
      --border: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
      --text: #e6e6ef;
      --muted: #9a9aad;
      --accent: #6366f1;
    }
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      background: var(--bg);
      color: var(--text);
      font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      font-size: 15px;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .wrap { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 56px 24px 96px; }
    header { margin-bottom: 40px; padding-bottom: 24px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
    header .brand {
      display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
      font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 18px; text-decoration: none;
    }
    header .brand img { width: 22px; height: 22px; border-radius: 5px; }
    h1 { font-size: 30px; margin: 0 0 8px; letter-spacing: -0.4px; }
    .updated { font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); }
    h2 {
      font-size: 19px; margin: 42px 0 12px; padding-top: 20px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border); letter-spacing: -0.2px;
    }
    h2:first-of-type { border-top: none; padding-top: 0; }
    h3 { font-size: 15px; margin: 24px 0 8px; color: var(--text); }
    p, li { color: #cfcfdd; }
    ul { padding-left: 22px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
    a { color: #8b8dfa; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 13.5px; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: 9px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
    th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; }
    code {
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;
      font-size: 13px; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    }
    .note {
      background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 18px 0; font-size: 14px;
    }
    .warn {
      background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;
      padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 18px 0; font-size: 14px;
    }
    .muted { color: var(--muted); }
    footer {
      margin-top: 56px; padding-top: 24px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
    footer a { color: var(--muted); }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <a class="brand" href="/login">
      <img src="<%= siteLogo %>" alt="">
      <span><%= siteName %></span>
    </a>
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <div class="updated">
      Last updated: <%= updated %>
      <% if (companyName) { %> · Operated by <%= companyName %><% } %>
    </div>
  </header>

  <p>
    This policy explains what personal data <strong><%= siteName %></strong> collects when you
    use it, why it is collected, and what rights you have over it. It covers the panel
    itself, the accounts used to sign in, and the machines managed through it.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Short version.</strong> The panel stores the account details you give it, the
    records needed to run your virtual machines, and a log of administrative actions.
    It does not sell data, does not run advertising trackers, and sets one cookie
    &mdash; the session cookie that keeps you signed in.
  </div>

  <h2>1. Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    The controller of the data described here is the operator of this panel:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Operator:</strong> <%= companyName || siteName %></li>
    <% if (contactEmail) { %><li><strong>Privacy contact:</strong> <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a></li><% } %>
    <% if (contactAddress) { %><li><strong>Address:</strong> <%= contactAddress %></li><% } %>
  </ul>
  <% if (!contactEmail) { %>
  <div class="warn">
    No privacy contact address has been configured. An operator must set one before
    this policy is shown to the public &mdash; <strong>Admin &rarr; Settings &rarr; Privacy</strong>.
  </div>
  <% } %>

  <h2>2. What is collected, and why</h2>

  <h3>Account data</h3>
  <p>
    When an account is created the panel stores a username, an email address, an
    optional full name, a role, and a password that is stored only as a salted hash
    (never in readable form). The time of your last login and an optional avatar are
    also stored.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    <strong>Why:</strong> to identify you, let you sign in, and decide what you are
    allowed to access.
  </p>

  <h3>Sign-in with Google, Discord or GitHub</h3>
  <p>
    If you choose to sign in with a third-party account, that provider tells the panel
    a stable account identifier and, depending on the provider, your email address,
    display name and avatar. The panel stores the identifier together with the email
    and name, so it can recognise you on your next visit. The panel never receives your
    password for those services and cannot read your messages, files or contacts.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Data received</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Google</td><td>Account ID, email address, name, avatar URL</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>Account ID, email address (primary, verified), username, avatar URL</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Discord</td><td>Account ID, email address (if verified), username, avatar URL</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Signing in this way is optional. The account is linked only when you initiate it, or
    when the provider states the email address is verified and an account with that same
    address already exists.
  </p>

  <h3>Two-factor authentication</h3>
  <p>
    If you enable two-factor authentication, the panel stores a TOTP secret and a set of
    single-use recovery codes. Both are encrypted or hashed at rest. The secret is what
    lets the panel check the six-digit code from your authenticator app; it is not shared
    with anyone.
  </p>

  <h3>Virtual machines and infrastructure</h3>
  <p>
    To run what you ask it to run, the panel records the virtual machines and containers
    you own, their resource allocations, IP addresses, port forwardings, snapshots and
    backups, OS templates, and the compute nodes those run on. Machine credentials for
    nodes you add are stored encrypted.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    <strong>Why:</strong> this is the service itself. Without these records the panel
    cannot start, stop, bill or restore your machines.
  </p>

  <h3>Activity logs</h3>
  <p>
    Administrative and security-relevant actions are logged: who did what, to which
    resource, from which IP address, with which browser user agent, and when. Examples
    are signing in, changing a password, adding a compute node, approving a node join
    request, and deleting a machine.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    <strong>Why:</strong> security and accountability. These records are what allow the
    operator to investigate abuse and to answer &ldquo;who approved this?&rdquo;.
  </p>

  <h3>Server and technical data</h3>
  <p>
    Requests to the panel carry an IP address, a timestamp and a user agent. These are
    processed to deliver the page, enforce rate limits, and keep the service available.
    IP addresses appear in activity logs and in node join requests.
  </p>

  <h3>Cookies and local storage</h3>
  <p>
    The panel sets exactly one cookie: a session cookie. It holds an opaque session
    identifier and keeps you signed in between page loads. It is
    <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, marked <code>Secure</code> whenever
    the panel is served over HTTPS, and expires after 24 hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are no advertising, analytics or fingerprinting cookies. Because the session
    cookie is strictly necessary to provide a service you have asked for, it does not
    require separate consent. Clearing it signs you out.
  </p>

  <h2>2b. Legal bases</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR, processing must rest on a legal basis. The panel relies on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contract</strong> (Art. 6(1)(b)) — creating your account, running your
      machines, billing you. Without this data there is no service to provide.</li>
    <li><strong>Legal obligation</strong> (Art. 6(1)(c)) — retaining invoices and records
      where accounting or tax law requires it.</li>
    <li><strong>Legitimate interests</strong> (Art. 6(1)(f)) — keeping the service secure,
      preventing abuse, and maintaining activity logs. These interests are balanced against
      your rights, and you may object to them.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent</strong> (Art. 6(1)(a)) — where you actively choose to link a
      social sign-in account. You can withdraw this by unlinking it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Strictly necessary cookies do not need consent under § 25 of the Belgian implementation
    of the ePrivacy rules (Wet van 30 juli 2020 / Loi du 30 juillet 2020), which mirrors
    Art. 5(3) of Directive 2002/58/EC.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Who data is shared with</h2>
  <p>
    Data is not sold, rented, or traded. It is disclosed only in these situations:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in providers</strong> &mdash; when you choose social login, the
      exchange with Google, Discord or GitHub is inherent to that choice. Each provider
      processes data under its own privacy policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Infrastructure providers</strong> &mdash; the servers this panel runs on are
      rented from hosting providers, who necessarily process the data stored on them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Upstream providers</strong> &mdash; if the operator resells infrastructure
      from another provider, the details needed to create your machine are passed to them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legal obligations</strong> &mdash; where the operator is required by law to
      disclose data, or needs to disclose it to establish, exercise or defend legal claims.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Content hosted <em>on</em> a virtual machine you rent is under your control and is not
    inspected by the panel. The operator's access to it is governed by the terms of service.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Where data is processed</h2>
  <p>
    The panel and its database run on the operator's own servers. Sign-in providers are
    based in the United States, so choosing social login involves a transfer of the
    identifiers listed above outside the EEA. That transfer relies on the provider's
    safeguards &mdash; the EU&ndash;US Data Privacy Framework where the provider is
    certified, or standard contractual clauses otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>5. How long data is kept</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Data</th><th>Retention</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Account and profile</td><td>Until the account is deleted</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Session cookie</td><td>24 hours, or until you sign out</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Activity logs</td><td>Retained for security review; deleted when no longer needed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Machine records</td><td>While the machine exists, plus any period needed for billing and backups</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Backups and snapshots</td><td>Per the retention settings in the panel</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Node join requests</td><td>30 minutes if unapproved; retained briefly after a decision</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Deleting an account removes its profile and linked sign-in identities. Records that
    must be kept for accounting or legal reasons, or that exist inside backups until those
    roll over, may outlive the account.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you have some or all of these rights:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Access</strong> &mdash; a copy of the data held about you.</li>
    <li><strong>Correction</strong> &mdash; fixing data that is wrong (most profile data is editable in the panel).</li>
    <li><strong>Deletion</strong> &mdash; erasure of your account and the data attached to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Restriction and objection</strong> &mdash; asking that processing be limited or stopped.</li>
    <li><strong>Portability</strong> &mdash; receiving your data in a machine-readable format.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdrawing consent</strong> &mdash; at any time, where processing rests on consent.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To exercise any of these, contact
    <% if (contactEmail) { %><a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a><% } else { %>the operator<% } %>.
    A response is normally given within one month. Requests are free of charge unless they
    are manifestly unfounded or excessive.
  </p>
  <p>
    You also have the right to complain to a supervisory authority. In Belgium that is the
    <strong>Belgian Data Protection Authority</strong>
    (Gegevensbeschermingsautoriteit / Autorité de protection des données),
    Drukpersstraat 35, 1000 Brussels — <a href="https://www.gegevensbeschermingsautoriteit.be" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gegevensbeschermingsautoriteit.be</a>.
    You may complain there, or to the authority in the country where you live or work.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Security</h2>
  <p>
    Passwords are stored as salted hashes. Two-factor secrets, node credentials and
    provider client secrets are encrypted at rest. Traffic should be served over HTTPS;
    the session cookie is marked <code>Secure</code> when it is. Administrative actions are
    logged, and actions such as approving a new compute node require a fresh second factor.
  </p>
  <p>
    No system is perfectly secure. If you believe your account has been compromised,
    change your password and contact the operator.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Children</h2>
  <p>
    The service is not directed at children and accounts are not knowingly created for
    anyone under the age required to consent to data processing in their country.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    This policy may be updated as the panel changes. The date at the top of this page
    always reflects the current version. Material changes will be announced in the panel.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <% if (contactEmail) { %>
      Questions about this policy or your data:
      <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a>.
    <% } else { %>
      Questions about this policy or your data should be addressed to the operator of this panel.
    <% } %>
  </p>

  <footer>
    <a href="/login">&larr; Back to sign in</a>
    <a href="<%= termsUrl || '/tos' %>">Terms of Service</a>
    <span><%= siteName %> <%= panelVersion %></span>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
